their windows are still full of  properties but agencies have had to  close shop and now the government's  effectively put the housing market into  the deep freeze  the director of this estate agents in  Southampton Tim Duggan says it's hard to  know what to expect next it's having a  huge impact we are still getting  inquiries in about properties which  we're just emailing back and saying  we'll look at it again in three weeks  when the restrictions been lifted we can  show you properties we are trying video  viewings but people still like to get in  and see the property before they'll make  a decision on whether they want to buy  it or if they want to learn what is  happening to the housing market is  unprecedented estate agents with decades  of experience are telling me they've  never seen anything like this before  the coronavirus has affected every  aspect of the market for instance on the  most basic level you can't show a  property and even if you could buyers  can't come and visit because of social  distancing rules and at this stage  nobody knows when that's going to change  many lenders have also now suspended new  mortgage applications but those who are  moving house should be able to extend  their mortgage offer for up to three  months enabling them to move in spring  or summer if the situation improves some  exceptions will be made  moving house will be permitted if it's  critically important or if the property  being moved into is currently empty  those who do go ahead though must  continue to respect social distancing  rules we have talked to the the more  lenders and we have agreed that they  would look to extend those mortgages to  a point at which completion can take  place we're also talking to the  conveyancing sector to make sure that  practically completion dates can be  moved forward Clem and Elaine Mason who  are in their 70s put their house on the  market in January and were hoping for a  quick sale but they're now resigned to  staying put f